\details{
This function creates a presentation-ready summary table from a
fitted "RiskMap" model, an assessment of fits or cross-validation results for
multiple models.
Use \link[=coef.RiskMap]{coef()} or \link[=summary.RiskMap]{summary()} when numeric
results are required for further analysis.

Numeric values have \code{digits} decimal places. Values that are compared, the
estimate and confidence limits of a parameter or one metric across models,
share one notation and one number of decimal places. When one of them is
smaller in size than \code{10^(-digits)}, they get as many decimal places as
the smallest needs for two significant digits. They are in scientific
notation when that takes more than six decimal places, or when one of them
is a million or more in size. Zeros and missing values don't count.

The table of a fit or its summary has
\itemize{
\item the regression coefficients with their estimates and confidence
intervals,
\item the parameters of the spatial process,
\item the variances of the random effects,
\item the parameters of the family that were estimated, such as the
measurement error variance of a Gaussian model.
}

The table of a cross-validation or its summary has a row for each model,
with its scores, their differences from a \code{reference} model with their
standard errors in brackets, and its \code{PIT_area} with the limit of a correct
model. For a model of binary outcomes the area reads "not shown (binary)"
and the limit is blank, because the area is not a check of calibration there
(see \code{\link[=assess_prediction]{assess_prediction()}}). When every model is of binary outcomes, these
two columns are left out.
}
